/// Controls whether Nutrient suspends rendering and text parsing while the app is in the background.
///
/// When the app enters the background, Nutrient pauses queued page rendering, document
/// precaching, and text parsing, and resumes this work when the app returns to the
/// foreground. This avoids large memory spikes and background CPU use on complex
/// documents, which could otherwise lead to the system terminating the app.
///
/// Set this to `@NO` if your app renders document content while in the
/// background, for example as part of a background processing task. This keeps
/// queued rendering work eligible to run while the app is backgrounded.
///
/// Synchronous rendering through `imageForPage(at:size:clippedTo:annotations:options:)`
/// does not use the render queue and is not affected by this setting. Prefer it
/// for isolated background renders when you do not need queued rendering to continue.
/// App extensions are not affected because they do not participate in application
/// foreground and background lifecycle transitions. Mac Catalyst apps are also not affected.
///
/// > Note: This also applies to render requests scheduled through the render queue
/// > while the app is already in the background: requests that require rendering stay
/// > queued and their completion handlers are deferred until the app returns to the
/// > foreground unless this setting is disabled. Requests served from the cache
/// > complete immediately regardless of this setting.
///
/// Expects a boolean `NSNumber`. Defaults to `@YES`. Set to `@NO` to disable.
@ffi.Native<PSPDFSettingKey>(
  symbol: 'PSPDFSettingKeySuspendsRenderingWhenBackgrounded',
)
external PSPDFSettingKey _PSPDFSettingKeySuspendsRenderingWhenBackgrounded;

DartPSPDFSettingKey get PSPDFSettingKeySuspendsRenderingWhenBackgrounded =>
